ABSTRACT: Large-scale cloud ecosystems create diverse manifestations of logs, warnings and unstructured threat alerts that clog the mainstream cyber threat intelligence (CTI) platforms. Currently used correlation and rule-based systems cannot convert this disjointed evidence into real-time intelligence that can be acted upon. In this paper, a large language models (LLMs) based cyber threat intelligence extraction model is presented and customized to elastic cloud environments where semantic understanding of rapidly changing workloads and distributed attack surfaces must be constantly maintained. The architecture uses the large language models to understand the raw security telemetry data, standardize multi-source threat stories and generate high-quality intelligence products in the form of indicators of compromise, attack paths, and adversary profiles. The cloud-aware orchestration layer maps the extraction processes based on the dynamic resource states and services dependencies so that contextualized intelligent output can be generated in scale without property interrupting the operational workloads. In contrast to previous CTI automation systems, the proposed architecture focuses on semantic reasoning between logs, vulnerability feeds, and incident reports to uncover obscure relationships not accessible to signaturebased systems. The resulting intelligence can be used to proactively defend and triage incidents faster and refines security policies based on adaptive models across multi-cloud and hybrid systems, which can support resilient and autonomous cloud security evolution. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0260] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of the International Conference on Computer Supported Cooperative Work in Design, CSCWD | type=Conference paper
TITLE: TTP-MAS: A Collaborative Multi-Agent System for Uncertainty-Aware TTP Extraction
KEYWORDS: Cyber Threat Intelligence; Dempster-Shafer Evidence Theory; Multi-Agent System; TTP Extraction; Computation theory; Distributed computer systems; Intelligent agents; Knowledge management; Knowledge transfer; Natural language processing systems; Network security; Collaborative multi-agent systems; Cybe threat intelligence; Cyber security; Cyber threats; Dempster Shafer evidence theory; Knowledge-sharing; Multiagent systems (MASs); Procedure extraction; Tactic, technique, and procedure extraction; Uncertainty; Extraction; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) plays a pivotal role in cybersecurity by enabling proactive threat knowledge sharing. Within this ecosystem, Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs) serve as fundamental behavioral patterns that allow defenders to anticipate adversarial actions. However, effectively extracting TTPs remains a persistent challenge because they are embedded in complex, unstructured natural language reports. Current approaches face significant limitations: discriminative models generalize poorly to unseen textual descriptions of known attack behaviors, while generative approaches based on Large Language Models (LLMs) suffer from hallucinations and lack interpretability. To address these issues, we present TTP-MAS, a hierarchical multi-agent framework grounded in Dempster-Shafer (D-S) Evidence Theory. Unlike conventional pipelines, TTP-MAS orchestrates a judicial arbitration framework. Within this system, a specialized Correction Expert acts as an adversarial auditor by leveraging 'negative evidence' to challenge potential errors, while a Chief Justice agent resolves conflicts to enforce global logical consistency. Experiments on AnnoCTR and TRAM2 datasets show that TTP-MAS achieves superior TTP extraction performance, outperforming the strong baseline TTPXHunter by an F1-Score margin of 6.65% ∼ 10.13%. Furthermore, TTP-MAS provides granular provenance mappings for verifiable and interpretable extraction. © 2026 IEEE.

