ABSTRACT: Modern cyberattacks are increasingly utilizing multi-stage, evasive, and automated approaches that are difficult to detect with standard rule-based monitoring systems. Security analysts must study massive amounts of diverse security records, which makes manual correlation and threat detection time-consuming and error-prone. This study provides an Agentic Large Language Model (LLM)-based cyber threat hunting system that analyzes suspicious security activities using contextual reasoning and adaptive investigative tactics. The proposed system combines agentic planning with LLM-based analysis to generate investigation hypotheses, correlate multi-source security telemetry, and map detected behaviors to the MITRE ATT&CK knowledge framework for standardized threat classification. Graph-based evidence correlation is used to identify relationships between entities such as hosts, users, IP addresses, and domains, allowing detection of coordinated attack patterns.The methodology also incorporates IOC enrichment and adaptive risk assessment to aid in prioritized threat response and analyst decision-making. © 2026 IEEE.

